WEBVTT

1
00:00:04.400 --> 00:00:09.090
Swap two steps in an algorithm. What
will happen? You can work it out.

2
00:00:09.310 --> 00:00:14.837
Think it through. Every step has a reason,
and a big task divides into sections.

3
00:00:15.337 --> 00:00:18.727
Every step in a good algorithm
is there for a reason.

4
00:00:18.947 --> 00:00:22.662
Boil the water, then pour it,
because you pour boiling water.

5
00:00:22.882 --> 00:00:27.665
Toast the bread, then butter it. Butter
in a toaster would melt and burn.

6
00:00:27.885 --> 00:00:32.437
Unlock the door, then open it,
because a locked door does not open.

7
00:00:32.657 --> 00:00:38.090
That reasoning is logical thinking.
It is how algorithms are created.

8
00:00:38.590 --> 00:00:43.466
Here is the smoothie algorithm from
lesson one. Six steps in order.

9
00:00:43.686 --> 00:00:47.587
Now make one change. Swap the
lid step and the blending step.

10
00:00:47.807 --> 00:00:51.894
Before you watch, predict. What
happens when the blender starts?

11
00:00:52.114 --> 00:00:56.340
The blender runs with no lid on,
and the smoothie sprays everywhere.

12
00:00:56.840 --> 00:01:01.298
The same thinking on another
task. Here is the kite algorithm.

13
00:01:01.518 --> 00:01:05.698
Swap the string step and the running
step. Predict what will happen.

14
00:01:05.918 --> 00:01:10.097
You run into the wind, and nothing
holds the kite. The wind takes it.

15
00:01:10.317 --> 00:01:15.658
Take the tail step out instead: a kite
with no tail, and nothing else changes.

16
00:01:16.158 --> 00:01:21.266
Getting ready for school is one big
task. Ten steps in one long list.

17
00:01:21.486 --> 00:01:24.737
One long list is hard to
follow, and hard to edit.

18
00:01:24.957 --> 00:01:28.904
So divide it into sections:
wash, dress, breakfast, bag.

19
00:01:29.124 --> 00:01:34.047
Every step goes into one section.
Teeth into wash. Shirt into dress.

20
00:01:34.267 --> 00:01:40.351
Four short lists instead of one long
one. Each section is a sub-task.

21
00:01:40.851 --> 00:01:45.913
Sections are easier to follow. Each one
is short, so you know where you are.

22
00:01:46.133 --> 00:01:50.544
They are easier to edit. Change
breakfast from cereal to toast.

23
00:01:50.764 --> 00:01:56.337
Wash, dress and bag do not change at
all. A change stays in its section.

24
00:01:56.557 --> 00:02:00.087
If you are late, you can ask
which section took too long.

25
00:02:00.587 --> 00:02:03.419
Logical thinking is
mostly about decisions.

26
00:02:03.639 --> 00:02:08.098
A decision is a point where a
question decides what happens next.

27
00:02:08.318 --> 00:02:12.404
Is it raining? Then take a
coat. If it is not, you do not.

28
00:02:12.624 --> 00:02:17.919
You make dozens of decisions a day.
Writing one down makes it an algorithm.

29
00:02:18.419 --> 00:02:21.484
Logic puts every step
where it is, for a reason.

30
00:02:21.704 --> 00:02:24.769
Predict what a change will
do before you make it.

31
00:02:24.989 --> 00:02:29.493
Divide a big task into small
sections. Each one is a sub-task.

32
00:02:29.713 --> 00:02:34.543
A decision sends an algorithm one
way or the other. Think it through.
